// Nail Technician / Manicurist license — a state-issued beauty credential.
//
// Read src/data/tracks/CONTRACT.md before editing. The sourcing rule is the product:
// every field below traces to a state board site, a state statute, or an official
// testing-vendor page, and 'unconfirmed' means no primary source published it.
//
// This is the widest-variance track in the catalogue. Required training runs from
// 100 hours (Connecticut, Massachusetts, and — per one compilation — the District of
// Columbia) to 750 hours in Alabama, and two jurisdictions barely license the work at
// all: Florida registers nail specialists without any examination, and Nebraska
// exempts natural-nail manicuring and pedicuring from licensure entirely.
//
// TWO CORRECTIONS TO THE WIDELY-COPIED 2024 HOUR TABLES, both confirmed on .gov:
//   * CONNECTICUT IS NO LONGER UNLICENSED. Nearly every aggregator still says
//     "Connecticut does not license nail technicians." The Connecticut Department of
//     Public Health states plainly that no person may practise as a nail technician
//     after January 1, 2021 without a DPH licence. It is a 100-hour, no-exam licence,
//     which is why the old "unlicensed" claim survived so long.
//   * NEBRASKA now licenses only ARTIFICIAL nail services. Natural-nail manicuring and
//     pedicuring were moved onto the DHHS "does not require a licence" list.
//
// Where a state's figures come from third-party compilations rather than the board,
// the entry is verified:false and names the compilation. An honest false is useful.

