  quiz: [
  {q:"Which content area carries the heaviest weighting on the National Interstate Council nail technology theory exam and accounts for the most failures?",
   opts:["Nail art and design", "Infection control, sanitation and disinfection", "History of the profession", "Client retail sales"], a:1,
   explanation:"Infection control is the single largest domain on the NIC nail technology theory exam and the most common reason candidates lose enough points to fail. It is also the area state inspectors cite most often."},
  {q:"A disinfectant used on metal manicuring implements between clients must, at minimum, be:",
   opts:["Bactericidal only", "EPA-registered, hospital-level, and bactericidal, virucidal and fungicidal", "Any household bleach spray", "A 70% alcohol wipe"], a:1,
   explanation:"Boards require an EPA-registered hospital-grade disinfectant with bactericidal, virucidal and fungicidal claims, used at full immersion for the contact time printed on the label. An alcohol wipe is not a substitute."},
  {q:"New nail plate cells are produced in the:",
   opts:["Nail bed", "Lunula", "Matrix", "Hyponychium"], a:2,
   explanation:"The matrix, under the skin at the base of the nail, produces the keratinised cells that become the nail plate. The lunula is simply the visible front portion of the matrix."},
  {q:"Onychomycosis is:",
   opts:["A bacterial infection of the nail fold", "A fungal infection of the nail", "An allergic contact dermatitis", "A harmless pigmented band"], a:1,
   explanation:"Onychomycosis is a fungal nail infection. It is a disease, not a disorder, so the correct action is to refuse service on that nail and refer the client to a physician."},
  {q:"A single dark lengthwise band on one nail that has been widening should be:",
   opts:["Buffed smooth", "Covered with an opaque polish", "Referred to a physician, with no service to that nail", "Treated with an over-the-counter antifungal"], a:2,
   explanation:"A widening pigmented band on one nail can be subungual melanoma. Nail technicians do not diagnose, but they must not conceal it — refer the client and do not service the nail."},
  {q:"Which of these must never be used on a client?",
   opts:["A fine-grit buffer", "Methyl methacrylate (MMA) monomer", "Ethyl methacrylate (EMA) monomer", "A wooden pusher"], a:1,
   explanation:"MMA bonds so aggressively that trauma tears the nail plate off the bed rather than releasing the product. It is prohibited for nail use by essentially every board; EMA is the accepted monomer."},
  {q:"Gel polish hardens by:",
   opts:["Evaporation of solvent", "Photoinitiated polymerization under UV or LED light", "An air-drying chemical reaction", "Heat curing in a dry oven"], a:1,
   explanation:"Gels contain a photoinitiator that fires when exposed to the right wavelength of ultraviolet or LED light, polymerising the product. Without the lamp, the gel stays liquid indefinitely."},
  {q:"Two-part liquid-and-powder acrylic hardens because:",
   opts:["Ultraviolet light triggers a catalyst", "An initiator in the polymer powder reacts with the liquid monomer", "The solvent in the liquid evaporates", "The powder absorbs water from the air"], a:1,
   explanation:"An initiator (typically benzoyl peroxide) in the polymer powder starts the chain reaction with the liquid monomer, so acrylic cures in open air with no lamp."},
  {q:"Whirlpool foot spas have most notably been linked to outbreaks of:",
   opts:["Head lice", "Mycobacterium skin infections on the lower legs", "Fingernail fungus", "Contact urticaria"], a:1,
   explanation:"Poorly disinfected whirlpool foot spas caused documented Mycobacterium fortuitum outbreaks, producing boils on clients' lower legs. This is why boards require screens to be removed, scrubbed and the tub disinfected after every client."},
  {q:"When using an electric file on a natural nail, the safest technique is to:",
   opts:["Run the highest speed to finish quickly", "Keep the bit flat and moving at low to moderate speed", "Work the bit into the sidewalls to clear cuticle", "Use a carbide bit intended for acrylic"], a:1,
   explanation:"Heat and rings of fire come from a stationary bit, an angled bit, or too much speed. Keeping the bit flat and moving at a controlled speed, with a bit rated for the surface, prevents the injury."},
  {q:"The natural nail should be filed:",
   opts:["Back and forth with a coarse file", "In one direction, from the corner toward the centre", "In a circular scrubbing motion", "Only with an electric file"], a:1,
   explanation:"Sawing back and forth separates the layers of the nail plate and causes peeling. One direction, corner to centre, keeps the layers intact."},
  {q:"A nail technician may cut living skin with nippers:",
   opts:["Whenever a client requests it", "Never — cutting living tissue is outside the nail technician scope of practice", "On the feet only", "Only while wearing gloves"], a:1,
   explanation:"Cutting living tissue, including the eponychium and callus with a credo blade, is outside nail technician scope everywhere. You may remove dead cuticle from the plate and reduce callus with a smoothing file only."},
  ],

  flashcards: [
  {f:"Matrix", b:"The living tissue under the skin at the base of the nail where new nail plate cells are produced."},
  {f:"Lunula", b:"The visible whitish half-moon — the front portion of the matrix showing through the nail plate."},
  {f:"Eponychium vs. Cuticle", b:"The eponychium is the LIVING skin at the base of the nail and must never be cut. The cuticle is the thin dead tissue it sheds onto the plate, and that may be removed."},
  {f:"Hyponychium", b:"The seal of skin beneath the free edge of the nail that keeps debris and pathogens out of the nail bed."},
  {f:"Sanitation / Disinfection / Sterilization", b:"Sanitation reduces germs by cleaning; disinfection kills nearly all pathogens on hard non-porous surfaces; sterilization destroys all organisms including spores and requires an autoclave."},
  {f:"Contact Time", b:"The number of minutes an implement must stay fully immersed in a disinfectant for the label's kill claim to hold. Shortening it voids the disinfection."},
  {f:"Onychomycosis", b:"Fungal infection of the nail — thickened, discoloured, crumbling. A disease: refuse service on that nail and refer."},
  {f:"Paronychia", b:"Bacterial infection of the tissue around the nail, red and swollen and often painful. Refuse service and refer."},
  {f:"Pseudomonas", b:"A bacterium that grows between an enhancement and the nail plate, showing as a green stain. Remove the product, do not re-cover it."},
  {f:"Onycholysis", b:"Separation of the nail plate from the nail bed, usually from trauma or aggressive filing. A disorder, not an infection."},
  {f:"Subungual Melanoma", b:"Melanoma beneath the nail, seen as a dark lengthwise band on a single nail that widens over time. Always refer to a physician."},
  {f:"MMA (Methyl Methacrylate)", b:"A prohibited nail monomer. It over-bonds, so an impact tears the nail plate from the bed instead of releasing the enhancement."},
  {f:"EMA (Ethyl Methacrylate)", b:"The accepted liquid monomer for professional liquid-and-powder enhancements."},
  {f:"Photoinitiator", b:"The ingredient in gel products that reacts to UV or LED light and starts polymerization — the reason gel only cures under a lamp."},
  {f:"Ring of Fire", b:"A friction burn ring on the natural nail caused by an electric file bit held at an angle, held still, or run too fast."},
  {f:"Credo Blade", b:"A bladed callus shaver. Cutting living tissue with one is outside nail technician scope of practice and is banned in most states."},
  ],

